{
	title: 'Find: ranked, streamed search over all sessions',
	spec: `The host answers a find query with results streamed in tiers, cheapest first, each tier sent as soon as it is ready and a newer query canceling the old one:
1. Session metadata: id (prefix "158-" matches every 158-* session), name, model, cwd. Instant, from memory.
2. User and assistant text, then thinking, then tool calls (input and description), then tool output, then commit cards, then other records. Commit cards are indexed as their card title (Committed, size tags, short hash, subject) and file lines. Other records are indexed as readable text (notices, inbox messages, questions, answers, commands, outputs); per-round usage, turn ends, file changes, continues, rate limits and resets are not indexed.
A tier is a block kind; score = match quality plus a recency bonus. Quality is the average per query word (whole word 3 > prefix 2 > substring 1; case-insensitive; every word must match), +1 when the whole query appears as a phrase, +1 when the whole text is the query (ignoring a leading slash, so commands match exactly). The recency bonus is 1.5 halving every three days: recent hits rise among equals and a recent prefix hit beats an old whole-word one, but an exact match of any age outranks every inexact one. Results carry session id and name, block id, kind, age and a one-line snippet around the match, and link to that block (task 0z addresses). Filters restrict kinds (user/assistant, thinking, tools, commit, other), and a query may say in:<kind>, model:<name>, cwd:<path>, since:<age>.
Index: a rebuildable projection in state/find.sqlite (bun:sqlite FTS5, porter-free unicode61 tokenizer plus a trigram table for substrings), never a source of truth: deleting it only costs a rebuild. "The host updates it as history lines are appended (find subscribes with history.onAppend, a listener list on history; no module replaces another module's functions) and", at startup, catches up per session from its last indexed byte offset in the background (task 7j: chunks, never blocking the loop over 10 ms). Blob contents are indexed too, capped per block (first 64 kB). Until the index has caught up, the tier falls back to scanning histories newest first, so results arrive anyway. /find <query> prints the top results as a list with links; Ctrl-F opens the dialog (next task).

The projection and history scanning run in a dedicated Bun worker, with bounded reads and small transactions: not even one unusually large history record or SQLite merge may block the host event loop. Queries and cancellation are transient per-connection protocol messages, independent of following a session; result batches replace one tier at a time (top 50 each), and a terminal done/error event leaves those results intact. /find merges tiers by score and prints the top 20, using stable host-relative links that terminal Markdown expands with the current web login code. Filters accept in:text/user/assistant/thinking/tools/tool-call/tool-output/commit/other/metadata, model and cwd case-insensitive substrings, since ages in minutes/hours/days/weeks (m/h/d/w). Blank queries show no results; filter-only queries are allowed.`,
	status: 'done',
	notes: [
		'Host API for 6m: send {type: "find", request: <fresh string>, query: <string>, kinds?: ["text", "thinking", "tools", "other"]}; no sessionId or open session needed. Omitted kinds enables all; [] enables metadata only. A newer find on that connection cancels its previous query. {type: "find-cancel"} cancels on dialog close, as does disconnect. Standard command id/ack applies. Replies go only to the requesting connection: {type: "find-results", request, tier, results, done, scanning?, error?}. Ignore a request not current. Tier order: metadata, user, assistant, thinking, tool-call, tool-output, other. Each non-done batch REPLACES that tier with its current ranked top 50 (not a delta); scanning counts histories still being scanned for this tier. done:true is a terminal status, NOT a replacement (results is empty); error is a terminal failure. Result: {sessionId,name,blockId,kind,age,score,snippet,href}; age is milliseconds, metadata blockId is empty, href is /<session>#<stable block id> or /<session>. Snippets are plain one-line text, no markup; the dialog highlights query words locally. Tier buffers let 6m preserve selection and append lower tiers; numeric scores are comparable across tiers, and /find merges globally by score.',
		'Implementation architecture: host find.ts dispatches to one dedicated Bun Worker (find-worker.ts); find-index.ts owns bun:sqlite FTS5 unicode61 and trigram tables, byte-offset marks, bounded history read buffers and 16-block transactions. Parsing even a giant ASON record and expensive SQLite statements happen only on the worker, never on the host event loop. Worker scan/index loops yield after about 4ms and between transactions; this is independent of 7j host startup work. The metadata catalog is loaded in background into worker memory using sessions.load/liveFile; open-session metadata accompanies searches, append notifications coalesce on a zero-delay host timer. No disk/history writes in the search projection path except state/find.sqlite. Substring queries shorter than three codepoints scan indexed rows; FTS candidates are always verified by the common matcher (every query word, whole word 3 > prefix 2 > substring 1; sum times three-day recency decay).',
		'Performance measurement (aggregate only, imported legacy corpus into disposable /tmp home, source read-only): 2,036 sessions, 339,432 history records, 889,146,406 history bytes; projection contains 370,770 searchable blocks. Cold indexing took 666,019ms (11.10min) during concurrent agent/test load. Host 1ms heartbeat: p99 interval 3.85ms, maximum 27.03ms (scheduler/load outlier, NOT a synchronous history/SQLite operation on the host). A warm restart/catchup took 1,909ms. After avoiding repeated per-tier stat calls, five warm runs: sqlite all-tier completion median 257.1ms (range 219.6–601.2), cache median 1,037.4ms (719.8–5,004.9 under overlapping tests), no-hit query median 96.9ms (76.9–108.8), text+model filtered cache median 122.3ms (102.7–154.0), tool-filtered sqlite median 92.1ms (88.0–106.3). Metadata first-batch medians: sqlite 5.19ms, cache 2.22ms, no-hit 4.38ms; queries excluding metadata first received a content tier after 56–91ms (text+model) or 46–53ms (tools). Rebuild cost is substantial for an 889MB home; queries remain available through newest-session-first fallback scanning and all heavy work stays off the host loop. No durable timing tests were added; three behavior tests cover rank/all-word filtering, projection/append/blob/rebuild/stable links, and unindexed-tail/cancellation.',
		'Post-rebase integration: keep the asynchronous Outcome return type added to host.act by 7j and retain hello protocol validation from clients (sg). The five find integration lines brought host.ts to 404 lines; its introduction was shortened without changing runtime behavior to retain the 400-line invariant. Query fallback offsets are captured once per query before SQL iteration can yield to the indexer; otherwise background catchup could advance the offset and hide records absent from that query cursor. Rewritten/truncated histories suppress stale projected hits and scan from byte zero; normal histories resume from their durable offset.',
		'Validation after rebasing onto e660e8b: ./test has clean TypeScript/oxlint and all find/convention/link behavior tests pass. Startup timing fails under current load (joining UI 193ms vs 150ms); an isolated detached origin/main baseline, without v8, fails the same check at 164ms. One full-run config watcher test timed out at 5s but passes alone. These are external/load failures, not waived find invariants.',
		'Session meta in sessions.state.open is a live-file proxy that postMessage cannot clone; the DataCloneError inside the dirty timer killed the host (30 Sep). find.ts sends plain JSON copies to the worker.',
		'Bug (5 Oct): Find showed "bad parameter or other API misuse" under its metadata results. db.query() caches one statement per SQL text, and search() pauses mid-iteration every 4ms; when a newer query (each keystroke, or another client) reused that statement while the older cursor was still open, SQLite returned SQLITE_MISUSE and the content tiers failed. Invariant: each candidates() iteration owns its statement (db.prepare, finalized when the iteration ends), so overlapping searches never share a cursor. find.test.ts covers interleaved iteration.',
		'Bug (11 Oct): Find failed for every query with "<history>:<offset>: Expected ..." after a session history was rewritten to a larger size outside the append path: the durable mark offset fell mid-line and catchup parsed from there. Invariant: an index mark never makes find fail; when parsing from a nonzero mark fails, catchup reindexes that session from byte zero. find.test.ts covers a rewrite under the mark.',
		'Recency used to multiply quality (three-day half-life), so a 10-day-old exact match kept 10% of its score and the web dialog sorted tier first: a fresh /help security command in the other tier sank below hundreds of weaker user-message hits. Additive recency plus phrase/exact bonuses and a global score sort restore the invariant: an exact match of any age outranks every inexact one.',
		'Commit cards were indexed as their stored text (hash, subject, files) while the screen shows Committed (+N LOC) from the title, so searching commit or committed never hit a commit card; plumbing records were indexed as raw ASON and matched field names. Invariant: a card is findable by the text its title shows.',
		'The client rejected commit result batches because event-check.ts kept its own copy of the tier list without commit, so a commits-only search stayed at Searching forever. Invariant: the wire check validates tiers against findQuery.tiers, the one tier list.',
		'Commit rows read Commit <hash> (<subject>): <tags>, rank newest first (score 4 plus the recency bonus, whatever the match quality), and show each hash once, preferring the committing session card over copies that warn the session did not change it. An empty query with only the commits filter lists the newest commits. A full rebuild of 624 sessions took 113 s.'
	],
	needs: ['gj', 'x0']
}
